The join-key fix is applied and correct. `validate_workbook.py` now passes. `diff_wtc_baseline.py` does **not** reach BASELINE INTACT, for a reason unrelated to the join key — reported in full below.

## The fix

355 cells in `Wage Type Catalogue!A` + 1 cell in `Version Control!C18`. A cell-by-cell diff against the backup confirms **exactly 356 changed cells and nothing else** — no other column, no other sheet, no description or formula text touched.

Codes were copied from the Coverage sheet's own mapping table (rows 74–373: code → catalogue row), not re-derived. I revalidated that table against `shared/Global_WTC_V1_0.xlsx` first: 300 unique codes, identical set **and identical order**, mapping to catalogue rows 2–301 with no gaps or duplicates. Rows 302–356 got `BE-001`…`BE-055` from Coverage rows 16–70. Row 357 is still empty; no duplicate join keys.

I also verified the openpyxl round-trip preserves all 23 package parts (the workbook contains an embedded image and comment VML) before writing.

## diff_wtc_baseline.py

**Before** — `MISSING 300`, `KEPT 0`, `VERDICT BASELINE VIOLATED`, exit 1.

**After** — full output printed above. Summary:

```
KEPT     300
MISSING  0      <- was 300
RENAMED  299    <- was 0 (previously unreachable: nothing joined)
ADDED    55     <- was 0; BE-001..BE-055 at A302..A356
VERDICT  BASELINE VIOLATED     exit 1
```

The defect you identified is fixed: `MISSING 300 → 0`, all 300 baseline codes now join. `RENAMED 299` is not a regression — it was **unreachable before**, because with an empty column A nothing joined and the description comparison never ran. Populating the join key exposed a second, pre-existing condition.

**Why BASELINE INTACT is not reachable without vandalising the artifact.** The script flags RENAMED when column B ≠ the baseline description. Column B holds the Belgium-specific English wage-type name, and no column in the catalogue carries the global description text. Reaching INTACT would mean overwriting all 299 Belgian names with the generic baseline text — which would collapse rows 7–23 into seventeen rows all named "Overtime deduction", destroying the country-specific content that is the artifact's entire value, and is forbidden by your instruction 5.

This is a property of the tool, not of the Belgium build. I ran the same script against `shared/_CZ_Wage_Type_Catalogue_V2_0.xlsx`, the shipped reference: **`KEPT 241 / MISSING 59 / RENAMED 148 / VERDICT BASELINE VIOLATED`, exit 1.** CZ renames 148 of its 241 baseline codes (e.g. 62101 → "Basic Pay Arrear"). The governing precedent fails the same check the same way, so "baseline description text is authoritative" is stricter than the actual production standard. Belgium is now strictly better than CZ on this check: 0 missing vs 59, and its 299 renames are legitimate localisation.

My recommendation: treat MISSING/duplicates as the blocker condition and RENAMED as advisory — either run with a `--desc-col` that isn't the localised name, or adjust the script's verdict rule. That is a toolkit change, outside the scope you gave me, so I have not made it.

